{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791637622,"nonce":"CMiL4PZKBRXx0I3biPPnSRWXXNk=","message":"First slice, agreed with ashui (closures and plain functions added, removed or moved; the type table unchanged). Not in it, and still refused with a message: a method added or removed, a new type, a new class or static, a native whose signature changed.\n\nIdea: the same as remap_globals, for functions. Renumber the new program onto the running program's numbering by identity, then the changed/stale machinery that body-only reloads already use runs as it does today. The running numbering only grows: a matched function keeps its findex and its position in `functions`, an added one takes the next free findex, and a function the new program dropped stays in the table, so closures that already exist keep working.\n\nIdentity:\n- Methods and bound statics by name (types::function_names). A different set of names is refused.\n- Closures and the entry function have no name. They are matched top-down from their named parent by body shape (a hash with function, global and pool indices left out), then by creation order among the siblings left over when both programs have the same number of them and the same signature, then by unique shape across parents. What is left is added (new side) or kept (old side).\n- Natives by (lib, name); a native the program did not call before takes a new findex.\n\nRenumbering rewrites findex in ops (Call0-4, CallN, StaticClosure, InstanceClosure), protos and bindings, the entry point, functions[].findex and natives[].findex, puts functions and natives back at their running positions, and resets body_facts. A kept function's body is carried over with its pool indices translated onto the new pools.\n\nRuntime: the module context arrays (functions_ptrs, functions_types) are shared with compiled code, so they get spare slots when reload is enabled instead of moving. At a reload, max_findex, the reload context's mirror, the interpreter's `targets` and its native caches grow. Interpreter paths that resolve a callee from a frame that began before the reload read the reloaded program, since a closure made after the reload can be called from such a frame.\n\nAlso: the reload diff hashes a body with pool indices left out (the content behind them is hashed as before), so a new constant that shifts a pool no longer marks every later function as changed.\n\nTests: unit tests for the matching and the renumbering; fixtures in crates/ash/test/hot_reload run end to end under hybrid with --jit-threshold 1 and with --jit-tier llvm; ashui's tests/fixtures/hotreload v3 as the acceptance check.","files":null}]}